00:00:38.715 --> 00:00:39.590
So let's get started.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.220
So let's look at some of the
key features of Bigtable.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:51.330
Because Bigtable offers high
performance under high load,

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.430
large apps and workflows are
faster, more reliable, and more

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:55.681
efficient running on Bigtable.

00:00:55.681 --> 00:00:58.263
ALEXANDER ZAMUDIO: Bigtable is
ideal for storing large amounts

00:00:58.263 --> 00:01:00.130
of data with very low latency.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.020
Databases can automatically
and seamlessly

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.569
scale to billions of rows
and thousands of columns,

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:06.310
allowing you to store
petabytes of data.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:09.550
Plus, you only pay for the
amount of storage you use.

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.554
- Because Bigtable is a
fully managed service,

00:01:11.554 --> 00:01:13.720
you don't need to worry
about configuring and tuning

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.230
your database for
performance or scalability.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.100
We'll also create
backups of your data

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.870
to protect against
catastrophic events

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.359
and allow for disaster recovery.

00:01:21.359 --> 00:01:22.900
ALEXANDER ZAMUDIO:
Bigtable's offered

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.450
through the same open
source industry standard API

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:28.850
as HBase, the native database
by the Apache Hadoop project.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:31.360
The HBase compatible
interface provided by Bigtable

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.890
supports movement
of applications

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.560
between HBase and Bigtable.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.480
VANESSA STARK: As Bigtable
as part of the GCP ecosystem,

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.000
it can interact
with other services

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.590
and third-party clients.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.755
In terms of security,
all data in Bigtable

00:01:43.755 --> 00:01:46.030
is encrypted both
in-flight and at rest,

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:48.610
while access to Bigtable
data is easily controlled

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.140
through IAM permissions.

00:01:54.324 --> 00:01:55.990
ALEXANDER ZAMUDIO:
In this hands-on lab,

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.910
you'll use the CBT
command line utility

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:00.100
to connect to a Cloud
Bigtable instance,

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.559
and read and write
data in a table.

00:02:01.559 --> 00:02:03.266
VANESSA STARK: You
can check out the link

00:02:03.266 --> 00:02:04.620
to start the QuickLab here.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.479
You will need to set aside
about 30 minutes to complete it.

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:09.020
ALEXANDER ZAMUDIO:
So far in the lab,

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.890
we've created a Cloud Bigtable
instance and configured CBT

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:13.900
to use your project in
the instance by modifying

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.600
the .cbtrc File.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.380
We create a table My-Table.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:37.420
We list the tables to
verify My-Table was created.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.050
We add a column,
Family Name, CF1.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:43.590
We list the column families
to verify CF1 was created.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:47.400
We put the value,
test-value, in row R1,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.810
using the column family CF1
and the column qualifier C1.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:53.620
We read the data
added to the table.

